## Notes & Quirks

- **MARS detections are not comprehensive inventories** — the system focuses on large, persistent, and confidently attributable emission events detectable by satellite. Smaller or diffuse sources (e.g., distributed agricultural emissions) are underrepresented in the point-source data.
- **Publication lag:** Detections appear on the platform approximately 30 days post-detection; government and company notifications are sent 45–75 days post-detection. Real-time monitoring is not available to the public.
- **Sectoral coverage is expanding:** Oil & gas has the longest and most complete record; the Steel & Met Coal sector (Steel Methane Programme) is newer and still in beta; agriculture and waste coverage continues to grow via science studies.
- **Source type attribution varies in confidence:** Some records show "generic" source types (e.g., "Oil and gas facility (generic)") where high-resolution attribution was not possible; others are attributed to a specific facility type (flare, venting shaft, abandoned well).
- The platform has a companion analytical tool — **Eye on Methane // Insights** — for deeper country and sector analysis beyond the map interface.
- The **REMEDI** (Repository of Empirical Methane Emissions Data for Integration) database is accessible via the menu and aggregates cross-sector empirical measurement data for research integration.
- Data is downloadable in **CSV, Excel, and GeoJSON**, and programmatically accessible via **API** — making it well-suited for integration into R or Python workflows.
- For reproducible research: record the access date, filter parameters (sector, date range, country), and note that the dataset is updated weekly.

